.SH FILESYSTEM WATCHER

.TP
.B richmack watch start
Start and enable the incremental RichmackOS filesystem watcher.

.TP
.B richmack watch stop
Stop the watcher.

.TP
.B richmack watch restart
Restart the watcher.

.TP
.B richmack watch status
Display systemd status for the watcher.

.TP
.B richmack watch log
Display recent watcher events.

.TP
.B richmack watch follow
Follow watcher events live through the systemd journal.

The watcher uses Linux inotify and updates the RichmackOS SQLite file index
incrementally.

The watcher does not move, rename, or delete user files.

High-churn and sensitive directories such as .git, .cache, .ssh, .config,
node_modules, virtual environments, and RichmackOS internal directories are
excluded.

The service name is:

.EX
richmack-watch.service
.EE

.SH AUTOMATIC RAG INDEXING

The RichmackOS filesystem watcher automatically sends supported changed
documents to the Richmack RAG index.

Supported extensions include:

.EX
.txt
.md
.markdown
.rst
.csv
.json
.yaml
.yml
.html
.EE

When a supported file is created or modified, RichmackOS first updates the
filesystem SQLite index and then executes:

.EX
richmackrag index FILE
.EE

Embedding computation is performed remotely through the Ollama service on
richmack.local.

PDF files are not automatically indexed until PDF text extraction support is
enabled.

.SH KNOWLEDGE INBOX

RichmackOS uses the following recommended directory for automatic document
ingestion:

.EX
~/Knowledge-Inbox
.EE

Documents placed in this directory are detected by the incremental
filesystem watcher.

Supported text-based documents are automatically submitted to
.B richmackrag
for indexing.

Supported automatic extensions currently include:

.EX
.txt
.md
.markdown
.rst
.csv
.json
.yaml
.yml
.html
.EE

.SH DOCUMENT INGESTION WORKFLOW

The normal workflow is:

.EX
Remote Computer
      |
      | scp / rsync
      v
~/Knowledge-Inbox
      |
      v
inotify watcher
      |
      +----> RichmackOS filesystem SQLite index
      |
      v
richmackrag index
      |
      v
text chunks
      |
      v
richmack.local embedding API
      |
      v
vectors stored on Debian
      |
      v
richmack rag QUESTION
      |
      v
Granite response with references
.EE

.SH SENDING DOCUMENTS

From another machine, a document can be copied to the Debian server with:

.EX
scp ~/Desktop/aws-notes.txt \
    wisdom@192.168.1.78:~/Knowledge-Inbox/
.EE

Multiple files:

.EX
scp ~/Documents/*.txt \
    wisdom@192.168.1.78:~/Knowledge-Inbox/
.EE

A directory:

.EX
scp -r ~/Documents/DevOps \
    wisdom@192.168.1.78:~/Knowledge-Inbox/
.EE

For repeated synchronization, use rsync:

.EX
rsync -av \
    ~/Documents/DevOps/ \
    wisdom@192.168.1.78:~/Knowledge-Inbox/DevOps/
.EE

.SH VERIFYING AUTOMATIC INDEXING

View watcher activity:

.EX
richmack watch log
.EE

Search the normal file index:

.EX
richmack search aws-notes
.EE

View RAG statistics:

.EX
richmackrag stats
.EE

Inspect semantic retrieval:

.EX
richmackrag search "IAM permissions"
.EE

Ask the indexed document collection:

.EX
richmack rag "What do my documents say about IAM permissions?"
.EE

.SH MANUAL DOCUMENT INDEXING

Files already present on the server may be manually indexed with:

.EX
richmackrag index ~/Documents
.EE

or:

.EX
richmackrag index ~/Knowledge-Inbox
.EE

.SH DAILY WORKFLOW

A typical RichmackOS knowledge workflow is:

.EX
1. Send documents to ~/Knowledge-Inbox
2. Watcher detects new or changed files
3. Filesystem metadata is updated
4. Supported documents are RAG indexed
5. Embeddings are generated on richmack.local
6. Vectors remain stored on Debian
7. Query with richmack rag
8. Review references and Evidence Match
.EE

.SH AI AND RAG COMMANDS

Use ordinary AI without document grounding:

.EX
richmack ai "Explain Linux namespaces."
.EE

Use interactive AI:

.EX
richmack chat
.EE

Use indexed documents:

.EX
richmack rag "What do my documents say about Linux namespaces?"
.EE

.SH PDF INGESTION

PDF files are not yet automatically ingested into the RAG database.

The current RAG implementation directly reads text-based files.

Future PDF support can use
.BR pdftotext (1)
before chunking and embedding.
